{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_011CeCxEmtT4cQqvVsGniFwp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XMLGNsfcaeELPBNxi17gvJ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"artifacts/deck-review-acmecorp/handoff/20260819T230338Z/checklist_output.json","content":"{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "purpose_clear",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The title slide states \"Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders\" — a single sentence naming the category (developer tools) and the ICP (indie SaaS founders). However, it includes no quantified outcome. Sequoia recommends a declarative sentence in the format \"[Category] for [ICP] that delivers [quantified outcome]\" — the outcome half is missing here.",
      "notes": "Rewrite the purpose statement to include a measurable outcome — for example, \"Acmecorp cuts developer tooling setup from 20+ hours to one command, for indie SaaS founders\" — so an investor can repeat the company's value in one sentence after 10 seconds."
    },
    {
      "id": "headlines_carry_story",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "Mixed across the six slides. Slides 2 (\"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration\") and 4 (\"$8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth\") carry data-backed conclusions. Slides 1, 3, and 5 use topic labels: \"Acmecorp — AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders,\" \"One CLI wrapping GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel,\" and \"2 co-founders, ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product).\" DocSend and Sequoia both flag that investors skim headlines first and each headline should be a conclusion, not a label.",
      "notes": "Convert the title, solution, and team headlines into declarative conclusions — for example, replace the solution headline with \"Founders go from 20-hour integration hell to one command\" so skimming the headlines alone tells the investment story."
    },
    {
      "id": "narrative_arc_present",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The deck follows a directionally correct Problem → Solution → Traction → Team → Ask arc. However, five expected seed-stage sections are entirely absent: why now/catalyst, market sizing, business model, go-to-market, and financial projections. The standard Problem → Solution → Why Now → Proof → Ask arc (Sequoia, DocSend, YC) has significant gaps that leave core investor questions unanswered.",
      "notes": "Add the five missing sections (why now, market sizing, business model, GTM, financials) to complete the narrative arc before the deck goes to investors."
    },
    {
      "id": "strongest_proof_early",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Traction data ($8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth over 4 months) appears on slide 4, within the first four slides. DocSend data shows that if a reader reaches slide 4, 82% finish the deck — placing the strongest proof here is consistent with that finding."
    },
    {
      "id": "story_stands_alone",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The problem slide is a single sentence (\"Indie SaaS founders waste 20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration\") and the solution slide is similarly thin (\"One CLI wrapping GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel\") with no workflow, screenshot, or demo. Five missing slides (market, competition, business model, GTM, financials) leave major investor questions unanswered with no presenter to fill the gaps. DocSend reports investors spend ~2:30 of real attention on a share deck — this deck does not carry enough information to stand alone in that window.",
      "notes": "Add context to the solution slide (a before/after workflow or terminal screenshot) and fill in the missing sections so the deck answers the investor's core questions without a presenter explaining them."
    },
    {
      "id": "problem_quantified",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The problem slide quantifies time cost (\"20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration\") and names a specific ICP (indie SaaS founders). However, there is no customer evidence (quotes, interview count, or named examples), no cost of inaction in dollars or missed revenue, and no budget owner identified. Sequoia requires the problem slide to include concrete examples or customer quotes and to quantify time, money, risk, or missed revenue — time alone is not sufficient.",
      "notes": "Add one customer quote or discovery-call data point, and quantify the dollar cost of inaction (e.g., delayed launch = lost MRR, or 20+ hours/week at founder effective hourly rate = $X/week in opportunity cost) to establish urgency and a budget owner."
    },
    {
      "id": "solution_shows_workflow",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The solution slide states \"One CLI wrapping GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel\" — a feature description with no before/after workflow, no product screenshot, no demo link, and no outcome framing. Sequoia and DocSend require the solution slide to show a workflow change (\"before → after\"), not a technical description. YC requires focusing on value delivered, not architecture. No visual evidence was captured (input_format is text).",
      "notes": "Add a before/after panel or 3-step workflow diagram showing integration time with and without Acmecorp, and include at least one product screenshot or demo link to make the value proposition concrete."
    },
    {
      "id": "why_now_has_catalyst",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No why-now or catalyst slide exists in the deck. The six slides are: title, problem, solution, traction, team, ask. Sequoia explicitly requires answering \"Why wasn't this built before?\" with a genuine macro catalyst (regulatory shift, new platform primitive, behavioral change, cost curve collapse). The absence of this slide leaves the timing question unanswered.",
      "notes": "Add a why-now slide citing the specific catalyst — e.g., the rise of AI-native developer tooling (GitHub Copilot ecosystem, Cursor), a measurable surge in indie SaaS founder volume, or a platform shift that makes unified CLI tooling viable now in a way it was not two years ago."
    },
    {
      "id": "market_bottom_up",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No market sizing slide exists in the deck. The six slides include no TAM, SAM, SOM, or any sizing estimate. Sequoia and multiple sources require bottom-up sizing: ICP count × willingness to pay × adoption rate, with an expandability layer. An investor reading this deck cannot assess the size of the opportunity.",
      "notes": "Add a market slide with bottom-up sizing: estimate the number of indie SaaS founders globally (e.g., from Indie Hackers, ProductHunt, or SaaS community data), multiply by a realistic monthly price, and show an expansion path to adjacent segments (SMB SaaS teams, enterprise dev teams)."
    },
    {
      "id": "competition_honest",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No competition slide exists in the deck. DocSend reports investors spent 88% more time on the competition section in decks that resulted in funding vs. those that did not. The absence of any competitive landscape discussion signals either unawareness of alternatives or deliberate avoidance — both are red flags.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide mapping direct competitors (developer CLI tools, integration platforms like Zapier for developers), incumbents (each tool's native CLI or SDK), and the \"do nothing / build it yourself\" alternative, with 2-3 concrete differentiators for Acmecorp."
    },
    {
      "id": "business_model_clear",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No business model slide exists in the deck. While $8K MRR from 24 customers implies roughly $333/month per customer, no pricing structure, buyer vs. user distinction, or gross margin information is presented. Sequoia requires pricing model, buyer vs. user distinction, and gross margin structure — all are absent. For an AI-native product, a16z explicitly requires showing compute economics.",
      "notes": "Add a business model slide showing pricing structure (per seat, per project, usage-based), who pays, gross margin, and — since the deck claims AI-native positioning — a compute cost trajectory showing the path to 60%+ gross margins."
    },
    {
      "id": "gtm_has_proof",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No GTM slide exists in the deck. How the first 24 customers were acquired is not disclosed anywhere. Sequoia and a16z require ICP + channel (PLG, sales-led, partner-led) + early channel proof (pipeline, conversion, CAC, payback) at seed stage. The channel motion and acquisition story are completely absent.",
      "notes": "Add a GTM slide explaining how the first 24 customers were acquired (channel), the go-forward acquisition motion (PLG through developer communities, content, Hacker News, Twitter/X), and any early conversion or CAC data."
    },
    {
      "id": "team_has_depth",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The team slide identifies \"2 co-founders, ex-Stripe (eng) and ex-Notion (product)\" — directly relevant pedigree for a developer tools company. However, no names, photos, LinkedIn links, specific accomplishments, or founder-market fit storytelling are present. DocSend reports the team slide receives approximately 46 seconds (~30% of total deck attention). Sequoia requires founder-market fit to be demonstrated with specifics, not just employer names.",
      "notes": "Add full names, photos, and LinkedIn URLs for both co-founders. Specify what each built at their prior company (e.g., \"Led Stripe's developer onboarding tooling\" or \"Shipped Notion's API platform\"). Add a one-sentence founder-market fit origin story — why these two, why this problem, why now."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_structure",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The detected seed-stage framework (per stage profile) expects 11 sections: problem, solution, why now, market sizing, business model, traction, GTM, competition, team, financial projections, ask. The deck has 6 slides and is missing 5 of these sections entirely (market, competition, business model, why now, GTM) plus lacks financial projections. This is significantly below seed-stage structural requirements across Sequoia, YC, and DocSend frameworks.",
      "notes": "Expand the deck from 6 slides to at least 10-12 by adding the five missing sections — market sizing, why now, competition, business model, and GTM — and a standalone financials slide."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_traction",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The deck reports $8K MRR, 24 paying customers, and 18% month-over-month growth over the last 4 months. The seed stage benchmark (per stage profile) expects early MRR ($5K-$50K), 10-100 paying customers, and demonstrable MoM growth — all three criteria are met. Paying customers (not free users) validate real demand at the appropriate seed-stage level."
    },
    {
      "id": "stage_appropriate_financials",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No financial projections slide exists in the deck. Seed stage requires an operating model with revenue assumptions, gross margin, and runway. Carta and a16z require 18-24 months of runway planning to be explicitly shown. The ask slide states $1.2M but provides no financial context — no burn rate, no runway calculation, no revenue trajectory.",
      "notes": "Add a financials slide with an 18-24 month operating model showing revenue trajectory from $8K MRR at 18% MoM growth, estimated gross margin, burn rate, and runway post-raise."
    },
    {
      "id": "ask_ties_to_milestones",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The ask slide states \"Raising $1.2M seed at $8M post-money valuation\" with no use of funds breakdown, no milestone mapping, no runway projection, and no path to next financing or default-alive plan. The 2026 standard (Sequoia, a16z, YC) requires the ask to tie dollars → milestones → next round readiness. As the slide reviews note, \"A number without a plan is not an ask — it is a request.\"",
      "notes": "Rewrite the ask slide to include a use of funds breakdown tied to milestones (e.g., \"$1.2M funds 18 months: $600K engineering, $300K GTM, $300K infrastructure — targeting $30K MRR and 100 paying customers before Series A\") and state what those milestones unlock for the next round."
    },
    {
      "id": "round_size_realistic",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The ask of $1.2M is below the typical 2025/2026 seed range of $2M-$6M (Carta data, median approximately $3.8M). The amount is not dramatically unreasonable given $8K MRR and early stage, but it is below the benchmark range and the deck provides no contextualization (no use of funds, no explanation of why $1.2M is correctly sized for the plan).",
      "notes": "Either explain why $1.2M is correctly sized for the milestones planned (add use of funds and milestone mapping to the ask slide), or reconsider whether the raise size aligns with investor expectations and what the round actually needs to fund."
    },
    {
      "id": "one_idea_per_slide",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Each of the six slides addresses a single topic: title (company purpose), slide 2 (problem), slide 3 (solution), slide 4 (traction), slide 5 (team), slide 6 (ask). No slide attempts to cover multiple distinct concepts simultaneously. Note: the deck was provided as text (input_format: text); visual assessment was not possible."
    },
    {
      "id": "minimal_text",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "Word count estimates across all six slides are between 10 and 16 words per slide, indicating highly concise content. No dense paragraphs or small-text elements are described in the slide inventory. Note: the deck was provided as text (input_format: text); visual font-size assessment was not possible."
    },
    {
      "id": "slide_count_appropriate",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck has 6 total slides. The criterion sets a fail threshold at 6 or fewer core slides. Storydoc data shows approximately 10-slide decks achieve a 32% completion rate vs. 22% average, with a sharp drop-off after approximately 18 slides. A 6-slide deck is below the minimum for high-completion rates and, in this case, reflects the absence of five expected sections.",
      "notes": "Expand to at least 10 slides by adding the five missing sections (market sizing, why now, competition, business model, GTM), bringing the deck into the optimal 10-12 slide range."
    },
    {
      "id": "consistent_design",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The deck was provided as text (input_format: text); no visual rendering was available to assess color palette, font consistency, or layout. No design inconsistencies can be confirmed or denied from the text content. The producer script will apply deterministic gating for text-format decks."
    },
    {
      "id": "mobile_readable",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The deck was provided as text (input_format: text); no visual rendering was available to assess font size, chart legibility, or mobile readability. The producer script will apply deterministic gating for text-format decks."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_vague_purpose",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The title slide states \"AI-native developer tools for indie SaaS founders.\" The ICP (indie SaaS founders) and category (developer tools) are specific. However, \"AI-native\" is unsubstantiated — no AI functionality, ML inference, model usage, or AI-specific capability is described anywhere in the deck. a16z and YC flag AI positioning without evidence as a credibility risk in a saturated AI market, and \"AI-native\" without evidence functions as a buzzword.",
      "notes": "Remove \"AI-native\" from the positioning or substantiate it concretely on the title or solution slide — specify what the AI component actually does (e.g., \"AI-suggested workflow configurations,\" \"ML-based error detection\") so the claim is verifiable."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_nice_to_have_problem",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The problem slide identifies a time-cost pain (\"20+ hours/week on developer tooling integration\") with a specific ICP. Time loss suggests urgency. However, no dollar cost of inaction is quantified, no budget owner is identified, and no customer validation (quotes or discovery-call data) is provided. The criterion requires urgency + budget owner + cost of inaction; the deck meets urgency partially but not the other two.",
      "notes": "Add the dollar cost of inaction (e.g., 20+ hours/week at a founder’s effective hourly rate equals $X/week, or delayed launch equals $Y in lost early MRR) and cite customer validation to establish a clear budget owner and cost of not solving this."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_hype_without_proof",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The \"AI-native\" claim in the title slide is unsubstantiated — no AI functionality, model, ML inference, or AI-specific metric appears anywhere in the deck. The traction claims ($8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth) are specific and credible. The AI claim is the one unsupported assertion in an otherwise data-grounded six-slide deck; a16z and YC both flag AI claims without evidence as a credibility risk.",
      "notes": "Substantiate the \"AI-native\" claim by describing concretely what AI does in the product on the title or solution slide, or remove the claim to avoid raising a credibility question on the first slide an investor reads."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_features_over_outcomes",
      "status": "warn",
      "evidence": "The solution slide describes a feature (\"One CLI wrapping GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel\") rather than an outcome (what the customer achieves). The traction slide is outcome-oriented ($8K MRR, growth), but the solution slide — the one that most needs outcome framing — describes what the product IS, not what the founder GETS. YC and Sequoia both require the solution slide to focus on value delivered, not technical architecture.",
      "notes": "Reframe the solution slide around the customer outcome — replace \"One CLI wrapping GitHub, Linear, Stripe, and Vercel\" with a statement of what the founder achieves: e.g., \"Indie SaaS founders go from zero to a fully integrated dev stack in one command — 20+ hours back every week.\""
    },
    {
      "id": "no_dodged_competition",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No competition slide exists in the deck. DocSend reports investors scrutinize the competition section more than founders expect, spending 88% more time on it in funded decks vs. unfunded ones. The absence of any competitive landscape discussion — alternatives, differentiators, or \"do nothing\" framing — is a significant red flag.",
      "notes": "Add a competition slide that honestly maps alternatives (existing CLI tools, integration platforms, each tool's native SDK/CLI) and states 2-3 concrete differentiators for Acmecorp — do not use a generic 2x2 matrix without explanation."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_retention_rebased",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck claims \"AI-native\" positioning but shows no retention data at all, let alone retention rebased from Month 3 to filter novelty users (\"AI tourists\"). The traction slide reports $8K MRR and 18% MoM growth but no churn, retention rate, or cohort data. a16z requires AI companies to analyze retention rebased from Month 3 — the metric is entirely absent.",
      "notes": "Add retention data to the traction slide, explicitly rebased from Month 3, to demonstrate genuine product stickiness rather than novelty-driven early engagement — even a directional statement (\"zero churn in 4 months\" or \"90%+ monthly retention\") is materially stronger than the current absence."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_cost_to_serve_shown",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No compute economics, COGS breakdown, or gross margin trajectory appears anywhere in the deck. For a company claiming \"AI-native\" positioning, a16z requires showing the path to 60%+ gross margins and the compute cost trajectory. The $8K MRR is stated without any margin context.",
      "notes": "Add gross margin data (current and projected) and a brief explanation of compute cost trajectory to the traction or business model slide, showing the path to 60%+ gross margins consistent with a16z’s AI company standard."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_defensibility_beyond_model",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "The deck claims \"AI-native\" positioning but provides no explanation of what AI is used, what model or approach underlies the product, or what the defensibility moat is. No proprietary data, feedback loops, workflow embeddedness, or vertical specialization is described. a16z requires AI companies to show defensibility beyond \"we use [foundation model]\" — nothing in the deck addresses this.",
      "notes": "Add a defensibility explanation to the solution or competition slide — specifically, what proprietary data or workflow embeddedness makes Acmecorp’s AI component hard to replicate by a competitor using the same underlying model."
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_responsible_controls",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No mention of responsible AI practices, privacy, data handling, compliance (SOC2, HIPAA), safety controls, or monitoring appears anywhere in the deck. a16z notes that addressing these reduces deal friction, especially for enterprise-facing AI products. For a developer tool that integrates with GitHub, Stripe, and Vercel, data security is a core buyer concern that is entirely unaddressed.",
      "notes": "Add a brief mention of data handling practices and compliance roadmap (e.g., SOC2 Type II planned, no customer code stored, encryption in transit) to the solution or ask slide."
    },
    {
      "id": "numbers_consistent",
      "status": "pass",
      "evidence": "The deck states $8K MRR, 24 paying customers, 18% MoM growth over 4 months, $1.2M raise, and $8M post-money valuation. The implied average revenue per customer ($8K ÷ 24 = ~$333/month) is internally consistent with the stated MRR and customer count. No contradictions detected across the six slides. The reconciliation process found no disagreements between stated figures."
    },
    {
      "id": "data_room_ready",
      "status": "fail",
      "evidence": "No mention of a data room, cap table, historical P&L, burn rate, or any supporting diligence materials appears anywhere in the deck. The deck provides no signal that diligence materials exist or are available. a16z’s data room guide requires cap table, historical P&L, and burn rate as baseline diligence items referenced or implied in the deck.",
